//
// A view whose container is itself a view names a place inside the OUTER container, so
// reassigning that outer container ends it and the binding materialises (loft#1393).
//
// `formal/binding.md` `(B-Disturb)` ends the place a view names when its CONTAINER is
// disturbed, and `(B-View)` materialises a view live across one — a copy taken at the bind,
// and the author told.  A chain names ONE place however many statements it is spelled over,
// and `scopes::base_container_place` resolves a chain inside one EXPRESSION: `prev =
// dv.tiles.proto` names `dv`, so reassigning `dv` copies and says so.  Split through a local
// it did not — `t = dv.tiles; prev = t.proto` recorded `prev` as a view of `t`, the
// disturbance named `dv`, and `prev` was never shaken:
//
//   prev: vector<integer> = [];
//   for k in 1..4 { dv = derive(k); read(prev); t = dv.tiles; prev = t.proto; }
//
// `prev` read THIS pass's data instead of the previous pass's, on both backends, with no
// advice on either — which is worse than a wrong number, because the loop was comparing
// consecutive steps and so answered *nothing changed* on every step.  That reads as a result
// about the program rather than as a broken measurement.
//
// The cure is in the walk's own table: `ViewWalk::resolve_view_root` follows the container
// through the views already open and keeps the OUTERMOST field, which is the rule
// `base_container_place` states for a chain inside one expression.  It is a lower bound like
// everything else in this walk — bounded, and stopping at the first container that is not an
// open view.
//
// The resolution stops at a COMPILER-GENERATED container, and that boundary is not cosmetic:
// a vector local is bound to its own backing store (`v = OpGetField(__vdb_1, 0, …)`), so it is
// an open "view" of a local nothing in the program can disturb.  Following it moved
// `c = &v[0]` off `v` and onto `__vdb_1`, and the `(B-Ref-Reshape)` refusal for `v.remove(2)`
// under a live link stopped firing — caught by `tests/parse_errors.rs`'"'"'s three
// `b_ref_reshape_*` removal tests, which are this file's refusal-side control.  A place is
// only inside a container the author can reassign.
//
// The CONTROLS pin what must not move, and all three were measured green on the build this
// guard was written to catch: the one-expression spelling; a struct-typed tail one step deep,
// whose container is the local itself; and a SCALAR tail, which is copied by value, names no
// place, and must not gain a materialise.
//
// NOT covered, and wrong for a second reason: the same chain whose tail is a value BRANCH
// (`prev = if k > 0 { t.proto } else { [] }`).  `ViewWalk::leaf` takes a `Set` whose
// right-hand side is a value branch WHOLE, so no view is recorded at all — the ordering half
// of the walk, loft#1394's territory, measured unchanged by this fix.
//
// @falsified-at: 00ff5bb5 — interpret exit 1 -> 0 with 3 assertion failures -> 0, native
//   exit 1 -> 0 with 1 -> 0 (native stops at the first failure).  The three failing cells are
//   `test_a_two_step_chain`, `test_a_three_step_chain` and `test_a_chain_inside_an_if_arm`,
//   each reading the CURRENT pass's value — `-1,20,30,` where `-1,10,20,` is right — with no
//   advice.  The three controls passed there and pass here.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees,
//   measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against 00ff5bb5; falsify prints only the
//   channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both. The
//   assertions/exit channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not scored
//   in that run.

struct Tiles { proto: vector<integer>, n: integer }
struct Derived { tiles: Tiles, covered: integer }
struct Deep { d: Derived, m: integer }

fn derive(k: integer) -> Derived {
  Derived{tiles: Tiles{proto: [k * 10, k * 10 + 1], n: k}, covered: k * 320}
}

fn deep(k: integer) -> Deep { Deep{d: derive(k), m: k} }

fn test_a_two_step_chain() {
  seen = "";
  prev: vector<integer> = [];
  for k in 1..4 {
    dv = derive(k);
    seen += "{prev[0] ?? -1},";
    t = dv.tiles;
    prev = t.proto;
  }
  assert(seen == "-1,10,20,", "two-step: got {seen}, want -1,10,20,");
}

fn test_a_three_step_chain() {
  seen = "";
  prev: vector<integer> = [];
  for k in 1..4 {
    dv = deep(k);
    seen += "{prev[0] ?? -1},";
    a = dv.d;
    b = a.tiles;
    prev = b.proto;
  }
  assert(seen == "-1,10,20,", "three-step: got {seen}, want -1,10,20,");
}

fn test_a_chain_inside_an_if_arm() {
  seen = "";
  prev: vector<integer> = [];
  c = true;
  for k in 1..4 {
    dv = derive(k);
    seen += "{prev[0] ?? -1},";
    if c { t = dv.tiles; prev = t.proto; }
  }
  assert(seen == "-1,10,20,", "if-arm chain: got {seen}, want -1,10,20,");
}

// CONTROL — a struct-typed tail one step deep views the local itself, and was already right.
fn test_a_struct_tail() {
  seen = "";
  prev: Tiles = Tiles{proto: [], n: 0};
  for k in 1..4 {
    dv = derive(k);
    seen += "{prev.n},";
    prev = dv.tiles;
  }
  assert(seen == "0,1,2,", "struct tail: got {seen}, want 0,1,2,");
}

// CONTROL — the whole chain in ONE expression was already right.
fn test_the_one_expression_spelling() {
  seen = "";
  prev: vector<integer> = [];
  for k in 1..4 {
    dv = derive(k);
    seen += "{prev[0] ?? -1},";
    prev = dv.tiles.proto;
  }
  assert(seen == "-1,10,20,", "one expression: got {seen}, want -1,10,20,");
}

// CONTROL — a SCALAR tail is copied by value: it names no place and must gain no materialise.
fn test_a_scalar_tail() {
  seen = "";
  prevn = -1;
  for k in 1..4 {
    dv = derive(k);
    seen += "{prevn},";
    t = dv.tiles;
    prevn = t.n;
  }
  assert(seen == "-1,1,2,", "scalar tail: got {seen}, want -1,1,2,");
}
